  "raw_text": "Step 1: Calculate the distance between points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\). Given that \\(CD = 140\\) meters.\n\nStep 2: Use the Pythagorean theorem to find the distance \\(OC\\) and \\(OD\\). Since \\(O\\) is the origin and \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are directly south and east of \\(O\\) respectively, we have:\n\\[ OC = OD = \\sqrt{140^2 / 2} = \\sqrt{9800} = 70\\sqrt{2} \\text{ meters} \\]\n\nStep 3: Calculate the lengths of the ropes \\(HC\\) and \\(HD\\). Given \\(HC = 150\\) meters and \\(HD = 130\\) meters.\n\nStep 4: Determine the coordinates of points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\). Assume \\(O\\) is at the origin \\((0,0)\\), then \\(C = (-70\\sqrt{2}, -70\\sqrt{2})\\) and \\(D = (70\\sqrt{2}, -70\\sqrt{2})\\).\n\nStep 5: Find the coordinates of point \\(P\\) on the line segment \\(CD\\). Let \\(P\\) be at \\((x, -70\\sqrt{2})\\). The distance from \\(H\\) to \\(P\\) will be minimized when \\(P\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(H\\) to \\(CD\\).\n\nStep 6: Calculate the distance \\(HP\\). The coordinates of \\(H\\) are \\((0, h)\\) for some height \\(h\\). The distance \\(HP\\) is given by:\n\\[ HP = \\sqrt{x^2 + (h + 70\\sqrt{2})^2} \\]\n\nStep 7: Minimize the total length of the new rope \\(HP\\). The minimum occurs when \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\), i.e., \\(P = (0, -70\\sqrt{2})\\). Thus, the distance \\(HP\\) is:\n\\[ HP = \\sqrt{(0)^2 + (h + 70\\sqrt{2})^2} = |h + 70\\sqrt{2}| \\]\n\nStep 8: Calculate the total length of the original ropes \\(HC\\) and \\(HD\\):\n\\[ HC + HD = 150 + 130 = 280 \\text{ meters} \\]\n\nStep 9: Calculate the length of the new rope \\(HP\\). Since \\(P\\) is directly below \\(H\\) on the y-axis, the length of \\(HP\\) is simply the vertical distance from \\(H\\) to \\(O\\) plus the horizontal distance from \\(O\\) to \\(P\\):\n\\[ HP = \\sqrt{(70\\sqrt{2})^2 + h^2} = \\sqrt{9800 + h^2} \\]\n\nStep 10: To minimize \\(HP\\), set \\(h = 0\\):\n\\[ HP = \\sqrt{9800} = 70\\sqrt{2} \\approx 98.99 \\text{ meters} \\]\n\nStep 11: Calculate the greatest length of rope that can be saved:\n\\[ \\text{Saved length} = 280 - 2 \\times 98.99 = 280 - 197.98 = 82.02 \\text{ meters} \\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{82}<|im_end|>",
